HST Office: rich ribbon tooltips. Hovering a ribbon button shows its name and shortcut in bold and, below, one
plain sentence saying what the command does (owner decision for 1.0.2).

- QuickHelpFlags::RichTitle marks such a tip: its first line ("Name (Shortcut)") is drawn bold, the rest is the
  description: one line when it fits in about 60 average characters, else two lines of about the same length
  (never narrower than the title, one line too when the title is nearly as wide; a little wider if a long word
  would push a third line). In a dark theme the tip takes the theme's menu colours instead of the system tooltip
  look, which stays light on Windows. HelpTextWindow draws it on Windows, macOS and the gen back end with the theme's tooltip colours;
  GTK shows it natively as markup (SalFrame::ShowRichTooltip, which falls back to the plain ShowTooltip on back
  ends without rich tips). The tip delay, position and hide timer are unchanged.
- NotebookbarToolBox (every ribbon toolbox built from a notebookbar .ui) answers quick help requests itself
  (hst::ribbon::RequestRichHelp): it takes the item's quick help text, adds the sentence of the item's command for
  the frame's module from HstRibbonTooltipTable.hrc (hst-ribbon-tooltip-descriptions.patch, generated from
  data/ribbon/tooltip-descriptions.json) unless the .ui already gave a second line (hst-ribbon-description), and
  shows it as a RichTitle tip. A command without a sentence shows its name only. Extended tips (Shift+F1) and
  item windows (font name box...) keep their own help.

